{"step_index":31,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-17T18:19:01Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\n**Types of Pain**\n\n* **Somatic Pain:**\n  * Arises from noxious stimulation of musculoskeletal, cutaneous, and deep somatic structures.\n  * *Superficial Somatic:* Originates in skin or subcutaneous tissues; sharp, pricking, well-localized with clearly defined boundaries.\n  * *Deep Somatic:* Originates in muscles, tendons, ligaments, periosteum, and joint capsules; dull, aching, poorly localized, and tends to refer to related sclerotomal structures.\n* **Somatic Referred Pain:**\n  * Pain perceived at a site distant from the actual primary site of somatic injury or dysfunction (e.g., cervical facet joint dysfunction referring into the shoulder girdle).\n  * Follows an embryological segmental or sclerotomal pattern without mechanical irritation or compression of neural tissue.\n  * Characterized by a diffuse, deep ache with no primary neurological signs (normal reflexes, sensation, and motor power).\n* **Neurogenic (Neuropathic) Pain:**\n  * Initiated or caused by a primary lesion, dysfunction, or mechanical irritation of the peripheral or central nervous system.\n  * Described as sharp, shooting, lancinating, burning, stabbing, or electric shock-like sensations.\n  * Follows discrete anatomical distributions (dermatomal tracts or peripheral nerve distributions) and frequently presents with positive or negative neurological symptoms (paresthesia, hyperalgesia, allodynia, hypesthesia, motor deficits).\n* **Visceral Pain:**\n  * Arises from the distension, ischemia, spasm, or inflammation of internal organs (thoracic, abdominal, or pelvic).\n  * Poorly localized, deep, sickening, dull, and cramp-like.\n  * Frequently accompanied by autonomic manifestations (nausea, diaphoresis, pallor, blood pressure changes) and visceral-somatic referral patterns (e.g., myocardial ischemia referring to the jaw/left arm; gallbladder referral to the right inferior scapular region).\n\n**Subjective Assessment of Pain**\n\n* **Location:**\n  * Pinpointing the exact anatomical site and boundary using a standardized body chart; distinguishin\n<truncated 3499 bytes>\n* Measured with a millimeter ruler from the left anchor to the patient's mark; documented as a continuous value from 0 to 100 mm (or 0 to 10 cm). Highly sensitive to small clinical changes.\n* **Numerical Rating Scale (N.R.S.):**\n  * *Format:* An 11-point segmented numerical scale ranging from 0 to 10.\n  * *Anchors:* 0 = \"No pain\", 1–3 = \"Mild pain\", 4–6 = \"Moderate pain\", 7–9 = \"Severe pain\", 10 = \"Worst possible pain\".\n  * *Administration:* Administered either verbally (\"On a scale from 0 to 10, where 0 is no pain and 10 is the worst pain imaginable...\") or visually as a numbered line.\n  * *Documentation:* Recorded directly as a discrete integer score (e.g., NRS: 6/10). Highly practical for rapid clinical workflows and routine reassessment.\n* **McGill's Modified Questionnaire (Short-Form McGill Pain Questionnaire - SF-MPQ):**\n  * *Format:* A multidimensional pain assessment instrument evaluating sensory, affective, and evaluative qualities rather than intensity alone.\n  * *Sensory Component:* 11 sensory word descriptors (throbbing, shooting, stabbing, sharp, cramping, gnawing, hot-burning, aching, heavy, tender, splitting).\n  * *Affective Component:* 4 affective word descriptors (tiring-exhausting, sickening, fearful, punishing-cruel).\n  * *Scoring System:* Each of the 15 descriptors is rated by the patient on a 4-point intensity scale:\n    * 0 = None\n    * 1 = Mild\n    * 2 = Moderate\n    * 3 = Severe\n  * *Indices Documented:*\n    * *Sensory Pain Rating Index (S-PRI):* Sum of sensory items (0–33).\n    * *Affective Pain Rating Index (A-PRI):* Sum of affective items (0–12).\n    * *Total PRI:* Combined sum (0–45).\n    * *Present Pain Intensity (PPI):* A 6-point categorical index (0 = No pain, 1 = Mild, 2 = Discomforting, 3 = Distressing, 4 = Horrible, 5 = Excruciating).\n    * *Visual Analogue Scale (VAS):* Standard 100 mm line included alongside the questionnaire for overall pain intensity.\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-17T23:49:01+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":32,"source":"SYSTEM","type":"CHECKPOINT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-17T18:19:01Z","content":"{{ CHECKPOINT 2 }}\n **The earlier parts of this conversation have been truncated due to its long length. The following content summarizes the truncated context so that you may continue your work. **\n\n\n# User Requests\nThe following were user requests from the truncated conversation in chronological order:\n1. ///Gangrene — \r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\nGangrene is the death and decomposition of body tissue caused by loss of blood supply, infection, or a combination of both. It commonly affects the extremities but may occur in other organs.\r\n\r\nEtiology / Causes\r\n\r\n1. Reduced blood supply\r\n    * Atherosclerosis\r\n    * Peripheral arterial disease\r\n    * Thrombosis or embolism\r\n2. Infection\r\n    * Severe bacterial infection\r\n3. Trauma\r\n    * Crush injuries\r\n    * Severe burns\r\n4. Diabetes mellitus\r\n5. Pressure and tissue ischemia\r\n6. Frostbite\r\n\r\nTypes of Gangrene\r\n\r\n1. Dry gangrene\r\n\r\n* Usually caused by progressive loss of arterial blood supply.\r\n* Tissue becomes dry, shriveled and black.\r\n* Usually develops slowly.\r\n* There is a clear line of demarcation between healthy and dead tissue.\r\n* Infection is initially minimal.\r\n\r\n2. Wet gangrene\r\n\r\n* Occurs when ischemic tissue becomes infected.\r\n* Tissue is swollen, soft and discolored.\r\n* Pus and foul-smelling discharge may be present.\r\n* Spreads rapidly.\r\n* Can lead to sepsi\n<truncated 2504 bytes>\n2. Investigations for Musculoskeletal Disorders BPT 3rd Year Physiotherapy | Easy-language university exam notes | 10-mark answers Reference: David J. Magee, Orthopedic Physical Assessment (assessment principles). Simplified study notes; not verbatim textbook content. Contents • 1. Blood Investigations and Markers • 2. Ultrasonography (USG) • 3. X-ray (Radiography) • 4. Computed Tomography (CT Scan) • 5. Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) Page 1 