{"step_index":44,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-17T18:42:30Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-18T00:12:30+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-18T00:12:31+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/final_boss_2.txt`\nTotal Lines: 265\nTotal Bytes: 19092\nShowing lines 1 to 265\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: 1. Neck Disability Index (NDI)\r\n2: Definition and Overview\r\n3: Developed by Howard Vernon and Silvano Mior in 1991 as a modification of the Oswestry Low Back Pain Disability Questionnaire.\r\n4: It is a patient-reported, condition-specific outcome measure designed to quantify self-rated disability and functional limitation in patients experiencing acute, subacute, or chronic neck pain (including cervical spondylosis, radiculopathy, and whiplash-associated disorders).\r\n5: Structure and Clinical Domains\r\n6: Comprises 10 itemized sections evaluating physical daily activities and symptom impact:\r\n7: Pain Intensity (severity of baseline cervical aching/pain)\r\n8: Personal Care (washing, dressing, grooming without exacerbating symptoms)\r\n9: Lifting (handling light, medium, or heavy objects)\r\n10: Reading (sustained cervical flexion/postural tolerance)\r\n11: Headaches (frequency and duration of cervicogenic headaches)\r\n12: Concentration (ability to focus on cognitive tasks)\r\n13: Work (ability to perform occupational duties)\r\n14: Driving (head turning, blind-spot checks, postural endurance)\r\n15: Sleeping (sleep disruption due to neck symptoms)\r\n16: Recreation (participation in hobbies, sports, and leisure)\r\n17: Scoring Methodology and Formula\r\n18: Each of the 10 sections is rated on a 6-point ordinal scale ranging from 0 (no limitation/no pain) to 5 (complete limitation/worst pain).\r\n19: Maximum possible raw score = 50 (when all 10 sections are completed).\r\n20: If all 10 sections are answered:\r\n21: \\text{Percentage Disability} = \\left(\\frac{\\text{Total Raw Score}}{50}\\rig\n<truncated 16756 bytes>\nints (Total maximum raw score = 96 points).\r\n246: Visual Analogue Scale (VAS):\r\n247: 100 mm horizontal line per item (0 mm = no pain/limitation; 100 mm = extreme pain/limitation).\r\n248: Total maximum possible score = 2400 mm.\r\n249: Percentage / Normalized Transformation:\r\n250: Raw scores can be reported individually per subscale or summed and normalized:\r\n251: \\text{Normal\r\n252: Score Interpretation\r\n253: 0: No pain, no joint stiffness, and fully preserved physical function.\r\n254: High Scores (up to 96 on Likert or 100%): Severe degenerative joint disease, advanced structural joint compromise, marked disability, and severe pain.\r\n255: A reduction in score indicates positive therapeutic response (e.g., following quadriceps strengthening, weight management, joint mobilization, or arthroplasty).\r\n256: Psychometric Properties\r\n257: Reliability: High test-retest reliability (ICC ranges between 0.86 and 0.95 across hip and knee OA patients).\r\n258: Validity: Strong face, content, and construct validity when correlated with radiographic Kellgren-Lawrence grading, joint space narrowing, and Lequesne Index.\r\n259: Minimal Clinically Important Difference (MCID): A 9% to 12% relative change from baseline (or a reduction of 10 to 15 mm on the normalized VAS score).\r\n260: Relevance in Physiotherapy Practice\r\n261: Forms the primary functional baseline before initiating geriatric rehabilitation protocols, aquatic therapy, manual joint distraction, or closed-chain lower extremity exercises.\r\n262: Widely used as the primary outcome measure in international clinical trials comparing conservative physiotherapy versus total joint arthroplasty (TKA / THA).\r\n263: Clinical Limitations\r\n264: Features a pronounced ceiling effect in young, active patients; it does not evaluate running, jumping, cutting, or athletic tasks.\r\n265: The physical function section contains 17 items that can be repetitive for individuals with mild, early-stage osteoarthritic changes.\nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
