{"step_index":46,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-17T18:42:56Z","content":"--- **1 Neck Disability Index NDI** • **Definition and Overview** • Developed by Howard Vernon and Silvano Mior in 1991 as a modification of the Oswestry Low Back Pain Disability Questionnaire • It is a patient reported condition specific outcome measure designed to quantify self rated disability and functional limitation in patients experiencing acute subacute or chronic neck pain including cervical spondylosis radiculopathy and whiplash associated disorders • **Structure and Clinical Domains** • Comprises 10 itemized sections evaluating physical daily activities and symptom impact • Pain Intensity severity of baseline cervical aching/pain • Personal Care washing dressing grooming without exacerbating symptoms • Lifting handling light medium or heavy objects • Reading sustained cervical flexion/postural tolerance • Headaches frequency and duration of cervicogenic headaches • Concentration ability to focus on cognitive tasks • Work ability to perform occupational duties • Driving head turning blind spot checks postural endurance • Sleeping sleep disruption due to neck symptoms • Recreation participation in hobbies sports and leisure • **Scoring Methodology and Formula** • Each of the 10 sections is rated on a 6 point ordinal scale ranging from 0 no limitation/no pain to 5 complete limitation/worst pain • Maximum possible raw score equals 50 when all 10 sections are completed • If all 10 sections are answered • Handling missing sections eg patient does not drive • Minimum completed sections required for validity 9 out of 10 • **Score Interpretation** • 0 to 4 points 0 percent to 8 percent No disability • 5 to 14 points 10 percent to 28 percent Mild functional disability • 15 to 24 points 30 percent to 48 percent Moderate functional disability • 25 to 34 points 50 percent to 68 percent Severe functional disability • 35 to 50 points 70 percent to 100 percent Complete functional disability / bedbound • **Psychometric Properties** • Test retest reliability High\n<truncated 14620 bytes>\ns 3 • Extreme equals 4 • Maximum raw subscale totals Pain equals 20 points Stiffness equals 8 points Physical Function equals 68 points Total maximum raw score equals 96 points • Visual Analogue Scale VAS • 100 mm horizontal line per item 0 mm equals no pain/limitation 100 mm equals extreme pain/limitation • Total maximum possible score equals 2400 mm • Percentage / Normalized Transformation • Raw scores can be reported individually per subscale or summed and normalized • **Score Interpretation** • 0 No pain no joint stiffness and fully preserved physical function • High Scores up to 96 on Likert or 100 percent Severe degenerative joint disease advanced structural joint compromise marked disability and severe pain • A reduction in score indicates positive therapeutic response eg following quadriceps strengthening weight management joint mobilization or arthroplasty • **Psychometric Properties** • Reliability High test retest reliability ICC ranges between 0 point 86 and 0 point 95 across hip and knee OA patients • Validity Strong face content and construct validity when correlated with radiographic Kellgren Lawrence grading joint space narrowing and Lequesne Index • Minimal Clinically Important Difference MCID A 9 percent to 12 percent relative change from baseline or a reduction of 10 to 15 mm on the normalized VAS score • **Relevance in Physiotherapy Practice** • Forms the primary functional baseline before initiating geriatric rehabilitation protocols aquatic therapy manual joint distraction or closed chain lower extremity exercises • Widely used as the primary outcome measure in international clinical trials comparing conservative physiotherapy versus total joint arthroplasty TKA / THA • **Clinical Limitations** • Features a pronounced ceiling effect in young active patients it does not evaluate running jumping cutting or at